Subclinical Hyperthyroidism
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Suppressed TSH (<0.4 mIU/L) | Strictly normal Free T4 and T3 levels | Asymptomatic or mild isolated tachycardia

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-023
Reason for Visit: Acute nocturnal palpitations, tremors, and severe anxiety requiring urgent clinical evaluation to rule out a new-onset cardiac arrhythmia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute resting tachycardia, tremor, and severe agitation. Immediate clinical review was mandated to exclude atrial fibrillation, acute coronary syndrome, or overt thyrotoxic crisis, preventing severe hemodynamic compromise prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My heart is racing for no reason, my hands will not stop shaking, and I feel completely wired and anxious.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports intermittent, subtle palpitations that acutely worsened tonight. There is a background of vague anxiety or nervous tension, mildly increased sweating, and subjective heat intolerance. The patient notes mild, unexplained weight loss and a mildly increased frequency of bowel movements over the past two months. There is an absence of overt exophthalmos or severe myopathy. The patient is deeply distressed by the sudden, intense palpitations preventing sleep.
Functional Status: Independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: High somatic anxiety.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 136/78 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 64 Kg
General: Patient appears agitated, diaphoretic, and hyperkinetic. Routine lab history incidentally notes an unrelated, stable diagnosis of incidental paraproteinemia, with an absolute absence of CRAB criteria and an M-protein <3 g/dL, which is currently monitored completely separate from this presentation.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals a slightly increased resting heart rate that is completely regular without murmurs or gallops. Neurological examination reveals a fine physiological tremor, particularly in the outstretched hands. Neck palpation reveals a normal, non-enlarged, and non-tender thyroid gland. Ophthalmic exam shows no proptosis or lid lag.
Environmental Observations: Patient has consumed multiple cups of tea trying to "calm down," likely worsening the hyperadrenergic state.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient’s chart notes a recent bone mineral density scan indicating mildly decreased density, congruent with prolonged mild hyperthyroidism.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Subclinical Hyperthyroidism - Status: Exacerbated by mild sympathetic arousal/caffeine.
Differentials:

Atrial Fibrillation - Ruled out definitively due to a perfectly regular, albeit borderline tachycardic, rhythm on auscultation.

Thyrotoxic Crisis (Thyroid Storm) - Ruled out due to the absence of extreme tachycardia (>140 bpm), lack of hyperpyrexia, and intact sensorium without delirium.

Acute Panic Disorder - Ruled out as the sole etiology due to the persistent physiological signs of hypermetabolism (weight loss, heat intolerance, tremor) independent of the psychological state.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low.

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient’s sudden onset of palpitations, tremor, and severe agitation necessitated immediate after-hours home assessment to exclude a life-threatening cardiac arrhythmia. Telephone triage appropriately identified the risk of acute atrial fibrillation or severe structural cardiac stress. Detailed clinical examination confirmed a regular sinus tachycardia driven by a profound hyperadrenergic state, completely lacking the criteria for a dangerous thyrotoxic storm. By executing this urgent assessment, an unnecessary, highly distressing emergency department visit was completely avoided, allowing the clinician to provide immediate physiological stabilization and secure the patient until formal endocrinological testing can occur.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Advised strict elimination of sympathetic stimulants. The expected investigation results include suppressed or undetectable serum thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) with normal free thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3) levels, which will assist in confirming the subclinical diagnosis via outpatient pathology.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce your physiological hyperarousal, immediately switch all beverages to ice water and actively cool your sleeping environment; lowering your core body temperature will directly suppress the excessive metabolic drive currently making your heart race.
Home-Based Services: None indicated tonight.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 3 days for a comprehensive thyroid function panel and ECG.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient’s heart rate becomes wildly irregular, if they experience crushing chest pain, or if they develop a high fever and become confused.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal thyroid function tests; endocrine states can transition from subclinical to overt rapidly.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course is fluctuating hypermetabolic symptoms requiring strict lifestyle management prior to eventual pharmacological or radioiodine stabilization.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency attention if you feel like you are going to faint, if your chest hurts, or if your heart rate jumps severely and stays irregular.

